  "response": "Economists approached the stagflation question by first establishing the timing and magnitude of the energy disturbance. The OPEC oil embargo against the United States began in October 1973, and that interruption of supply caused world crude oil prices to roughly quadruple between 1973 and 1974. A price movement of this size in a commodity that enters nearly every stage of production indicates that oil supply shocks raised US inflation during the 1970s. The same evidence, however, leaves room for a second mechanism. Accommodative Federal Reserve monetary policy raised US inflation during the 1970s. Much of the subsequent research has tried to weigh these two channels against each other rather than to choose one and dismiss the other.\n\nProponents of the monetary interpretation focus on the decade's earlier price history. On their account, the expansionary stance of the Federal Reserve raised inflation because US inflation had already risen substantially in the late 1960s, before the 1973 oil shock. Price pressures that predate the embargo cannot be attributed to the cartel, and a central bank that failed to restrain them carried them into the following decade. Supply-side scholars do not deny that earlier drift, but they stress how the energy market amplified it. In their view, the oil disturbances, compounded by a second round of price increases at the end of the decade, led US annual average CPI inflation to reach about 13.5 percent in 1980.\n\nThe output record is the second body of evidence in this adjudication. A pure supply shock should depress production while raising prices, so the behavior of real activity in 1974 serves as a diagnostic test of the oil hypothesis. Researchers working from successive vintages of national accounts have had to settle a basic question of fact. Either US real GDP growth was negative in 1974, or US real GDP growth was positive in 1974. The two readings cannot both be correct, and one of them must hold. Only after that question is settled can the year be counted as a case of contraction alongside inflation, which is the defining combination of stagflation.\n\nInstitutional chronology is the third element, because the leadership of the central bank determined how monetary policy responded to the second oil shock. Although Arthur Burns chaired the Federal Reserve when the Iranian Revolution began in 1979, Paul Volcker became chair of the Federal Reserve in August 1979. In other words, leadership of the Federal Reserve Board passed to Paul Volcker in August 1979. President Gerald Ford appointed Paul Volcker as Fed chair, and that appointment preceded the August 1979 handover. The disinflation that followed under Volcker is often read as evidence that a determined central bank could have contained the price level, whatever the condition of the energy market.\n\nThe scholarly debate itself is defined by two influential syntheses. Blinder and Rudd argued that supply shocks explain most of the 1970s inflation spikes, and they traced the timing of each surge to identifiable disturbances in the energy and agricultural markets. The apparent tension between Burns presiding over the onset of the Iranian crisis and Volcker taking office in the same year is settled by Barsky and Kilian. They concluded in the 2001 NBER Macroeconomics Annual that monetary expansion, not oil shocks, was the primary cause of the Great Stagflation, so the personnel question matters less than the persistent monetary stance that preceded both men's decisions. That is, Blinder and Rudd's review ultimately concluded that oil and food supply shocks, rather than monetary policy, drove the major 1970s inflation spikes. The adjudication has therefore rested on careful dating of prices, output, and policy decisions, through which each school has sought to assign responsibility for the decade's inflation.",
